      "rationale": "Issue672 (\"Ignore dunder attributes when creating Tokens\") ensured that _TokenType.__getattr__ raises AttributeError for dunder names so that copy.copy/copy.deepcopy's getattr(obj, \"__copy__\"/\"__deepcopy__\", None) probing gets None instead of a bogus, non-callable _TokenType instance that copy then tries to call, producing \"TypeError: '_TokenType' object is not callable\". The current tokens.py has the comment \"# don't mess with dunder\" but no actual guard, so the first two tests (direct dunder access and copy.copy/deepcopy on a token type) fail with the reported TypeError, and the third test reproduces the exact regressed scenario of deep-copying a parsed Statement. All expected values (AttributeError semantics, equality/independence of copies) come from Python's documented copy protocol and the tuple-equality contract of _TokenType, not from the buggy code's current output.",
